# Human Writing Tone (Blog, Site, LinkedIn)

Apply when drafting or editing articles on zanganehai.com, portfolio copy, and LinkedIn posts or article announcements.

## Voice

- Write like a senior engineer explaining something to a peer, not like a content template.
- Prefer plain sentences. Mix short and long paragraphs. Not every paragraph needs a punchline.
- Use first person sparingly. Some is fine ("I still ask…", "we have all seen…"). Do not announce your position in every section ("I would…", "I want…", "I believe…").
- OK to be direct and slightly informal. Avoid motivational or thought-leadership tone.

## Patterns to avoid (AI tells)

- **Repeated X vs Y contrasts** — "Lag is not age. Lag tells you X. Age tells you Y." Use at most one or two per piece, not as a rhythm.
- **Perfect symmetry** — incident → concept → table → checklist → "short version" → "what comes next" in identical shape every time.
- **Packaged takeaways** — highlight boxes titled "The core argument", "Short version", "Key takeaways" unless the piece truly needs a recap. Prefer ending in prose.
- **Over-bolded terms** — do not bold every concept. Let headings and context carry weight.
- **Series-summary voice** — avoid "as described in the earlier posts", "this post moves one layer downstream". Reference prior work naturally or link at the bottom.
- **Staccato fragments** — "Green. Capacity looked fine. Nobody paged." unless it is deliberate and rare.
- **Em dashes everywhere** — use sparingly. Commas and periods are fine.
- **Rhetorical stacking** — "not X. That is Y. That is Z." Cut to one clear sentence.

## Structure

- Open with what the reader will learn, in plain language. Do not assume they read the last post; one sentence of context is enough.
- Headings can be plain ("Paging", "Runbooks vs redesign") — they do not all need to be polished aphorisms.
- Tables: short column headers (`Measure`, `Why`, `Common substitute`). Use `<th scope="col">` per column.
- Checklists are fine for design review, but keep them short. Do not duplicate the whole article in bullets at the end.
- "Further reading" links at the bottom are enough for series continuity. No forced teaser section for the next post unless the user asks for one.

## LinkedIn

- Announcement posts: short, conversational. Not a compressed copy of the article.
- Article body: paste from the site or write for LinkedIn; same tone rules apply.
- No hashtag stuffing in the article body. Tags at the end only, if at all.

## Punctuation

- Standard professional punctuation. No excessive semicolons, parentheses, or em dashes.
- Use `&mdash;` in HTML articles only where a real break is needed, not as default clause glue.

## Before shipping

- Read aloud once. If it sounds like a syllabus or a LinkedIn influencer template, loosen one section.
- Cut 30–40% of "I would" and contrast sentences on revision passes.
- Technical substance first. Polish second.
